# Owner color-picker template

This application carries the owner-supplied color interaction as a reusable component:

- `app/src/ui/ColorPicker.jsx`
- `app/src/styles/color-picker.css`
- `tests/color-picker.test.js`

The behavior was isolated from the owner-supplied GPT-Mermaid theme designer. The
source project is provenance only; this application has no runtime or build dependency
on it, Tailwind, a CDN, or an icon library.

`main.jsx` loads `color-picker.css` through the application's global style entry. Keep
that import there: designer JSX intentionally accepts code bindings only, while the
travelling stage measures, copies, and links every CSS file in `app/src/styles`.

## Exact retained behavior

The component contains 22 Tailwind color families. Each family carries the exact
eleven shades `50`, `100`, `200`, `300`, `400`, `500`, `600`, `700`, `800`, `900`,
and `950`.

- The normal palette shows the `500` shade for every family as 22 circles.
- Left-clicking a family selects its `500` shade and immediately switches the
  always-present shade tray to that family.
- The complete lightest-to-darkest shade row is an always-present horizontal tray.
- Right-clicking a family changes which family's shades occupy that reserved tray
  without changing the selected color; neither action inserts or removes content that
  changes the outer picker or dialog dimensions.
- `Shift+F10`, the keyboard Context Menu key, and touch press-and-hold expose the same
  family-switching action without requiring a mouse.
- A valid three- or six-digit custom hex value becomes a reusable custom swatch and is
  selected immediately.
- Invalid hex input remains visible and names the rejected value, the required shape,
  why the picker needs it, and a valid next example.
- The designed opacity slider emits an explicit value from `0` through `100` and
  shows the exact percentage as text.

No theme name, canvas selector, border thickness, font selector, sketch control,
application tint, or GPT-Mermaid persistence behavior was copied.

## Controlled API

The parent owns product state and durable persistence:

```jsx
const [color, setColor] = useState({ hex: "#3b82f6", opacity: 100 });

<ColorPicker
  id="brand-color"
  label="Brand color"
  value={color}
  onChange={setColor}
  initialCustomColors={["#22d3ee"]}
/>
```

`value` is required and has the exact shape `{ hex, opacity }`. `onChange` is required.
Invalid controlled values throw a specific development error; the component never
fabricates a color or silently clamps opacity. `initialCustomColors` seeds only this
component instance. If custom swatches must survive reload or synchronize between
users, load and save that list through the application's real persistence path.

The component deliberately does not render a `<form>`, so it can live inside a
product-owned settings or persistence form without creating invalid nested forms.
Its custom-hex input handles Enter explicitly and its Add control is
`type="button"`. The product parent owns submission, busy state, durable save,
failure retention, and dialog closure. Do not add a form boundary inside this
reusable component.

Do not close a containing dialog or claim that a server-backed theme is saved until
the durable operation succeeds.

## Product adaptation

The component deliberately consumes the generated app tokens (`--surface`, `--ink`,
`--line`, `--brand`, `--danger`, `--focus`, radius and font tokens). Adapt those token
bindings to the product rather than replacing the picker or introducing literal
fallback colors. Preserve:

- all 22 families and all 11 shades;
- primary-click family selection and immediate shade-tray switching;
- the always-present horizontal shade tray, context-only family switching, and its
  keyboard and touch equivalents;
- stable outer popup geometry while the active family or shade changes;
- the exact selected hex and opacity text, so state never depends on color alone;
- designed hover/focus tooltips rather than native `title` attributes;
- the visible focus treatment and reduced-motion behavior;
- fail-loud controlled-value and custom-hex validation;
- the custom-themed range track and thumb; and
- the single `{ hex, opacity }` change contract.

There is no browser-stock `<input type="color">`. The opacity range uses a native
semantic input with its visible track and thumb fully themed, retaining keyboard and
assistive-technology behavior.

For a designer round, declare both `ColorPicker.jsx` and `color-picker.css` in the
designer surface. The test is engineering proof and does not become designer-owned
copy.

Visible Chrome proof must open the real containing form/dialog, exercise custom hex
with Enter and Add, change families and opacity, and confirm zero DOM-nesting or
other console warnings. A server-rendered component test does not prove composition.
